When a lightbulb produces light, it also converts electrical energy into another form of energy. Most lightbulbs, especially incandescent ones, produce heat as a byproduct of the energy conversion process. This heat is a form of **thermal** energy.  
The other options—chemical, magnetic, and mechanical—are not typically produced by a standard lightbulb during normal operation.  

\boxed{a}
